Excimer 1.2.7 has timers for Linux (timer_create) and for macOS and BSD
(kqueue) only, and no config.w32. This adds a Windows timer
(timerlib/timerlib_win32.c: a thread per timer that waits on a
high-resolution waitable timer and counts the periods it missed as
overruns, as the kqueue timer's thread does), a mutex that is an SRW lock
on Windows instead of a pthreads one, and config.w32. Wall-clock time only:
Windows has no timer that runs on a thread's CPU time, so EXCIMER_CPU isn't
defined there. Offer it upstream, and drop this once a release builds on
Windows.

diff --git a/config.w32 b/config.w32
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..a151449
--- /dev/null
+++ b/config.w32
@@ -0,0 +1,11 @@
+// vim:ft=javascript
+
+ARG_ENABLE("excimer", "whether to enable excimer support", "no");
+
+if (PHP_EXCIMER != "no") {
+	EXTENSION("excimer", "excimer.c excimer_mutex.c excimer_timer.c excimer_log.c", PHP_EXCIMER_SHARED, "/DZEND_ENABLE_STATIC_TSRMLS_CACHE=1");
+	// timerlib/ includes the application's timerlib_config.h from here.
+	ADD_FLAG("CFLAGS_EXCIMER", "/I " + configure_module_dirname);
+	ADD_SOURCES(configure_module_dirname + "\\timerlib", "timerlib_common.c timerlib_win32.c", "excimer");
+	AC_DEFINE("HAVE_EXCIMER", 1, "Whether excimer is enabled");
+}
